/**
 * Rural hospital closures and drive times (2026).
 *
 * Core question: Which counties lost their nearest hospital since 2005,
 * and how far must residents drive now for emergency care?
 *
 * Primary anchors: UNC Sheps Center Rural Hospital Closures database;
 * CMS Medicare Cost Report hospital addresses (provider of services);
 * Census ACS county population and rurality. County-level drive-time
 * deltas are desk GIS estimates (OSRM/road-network minutes to nearest
 * open acute facility pre- vs post-closure) ??? analytical ranks, not a
 * single agency publication.
 */

export type Confidence = "disclosed" | "estimated" | "carried";

export const SOURCE_NOTE =
  "Closure counts and facility status follow the UNC Cecil G. Sheps Center Rural Hospital Closures tracker (complete closures and conversions to non-inpatient status since 2005). Hospital locations for nearest-facility routing start from CMS Medicare Cost Report / Provider of Services address files for short-term acute and critical-access hospitals. County population and rural share use Census ACS 5-year estimates. Added drive minutes are desk road-network estimates (nearest open ED/acute bed before vs after the local closure event) for counties whose nearest hospital closed or converted ??? not Sheps, CMS, or Census publications. Minutes are road travel time, not ambulance chute-to-ED clock time.";

